  "prediction": "**John Lewis (TAO-205) Class Oiler Shipbuilding Program: Background and Issues for Congress**\n\nThe John Lewis (TAO-205) class oiler shipbuilding program is a Navy initiative to build a new class of 20 fleet oilers to support the Navy's operations. The program aims to replace the existing 15 Henry J. Kaiser (TAO-187) class ships, which have an expected service life of 35 years. The Navy's proposed FY2020 budget requests the procurement of the fifth and sixth ships in the program, with an estimated combined procurement cost of $1,056.3 million.\n\n**Key Issues for Congress:**\n\n1. **Procurement Funding:** Congress must decide whether to approve, reject, or modify the Navy's FY2020 procurement funding request for the TAO-205 program.\n2. **Number of Oilers Required:** The Navy's new operational concept, Distributed Maritime Operations (DMO), may lead to increased requirements for Navy logistics, including oilers. Congress must consider whether the Navy has accurately assessed the required number of oilers.\n3. **Ship Self-Defense Equipment:** Congress must decide whether to encourage or direct the Navy to build TAO-205s with more ship self-defense equipment than currently planned, considering the changing international security environment.\n\n**Program Status:**\n\nThe Navy has matured all critical technologies and stabilized the ships' design. The program office has largely kept to its construction schedule, but a flooding incident at a NASSCO graving dock in July 2018 has affected the delivery of future ships. The Navy plans to add two more ships to the low-rate initial production phase and compete a new contract for the remaining 12 ships using the construction knowledge gained from efforts under the existing contract.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nThe John Lewis (TAO-205) class oiler shipbuilding program is a critical component of the Navy's operations. Congress must carefully consider the key issues for the program, including procurement funding, the number of oilers required, and ship self-defense equipment, to ensure the program's success and the Navy's ability to operate effectively in the future.",
